{
 "id": "l02",
 "label": "2",
 "title": "Syllables and stress",
 "summary": "How to break words into beats, and why the stressed syllable matters",
 "minutes": 25,
 "goals": [
  "Recognise the four kinds of syllable and break words correctly",
  "Know the four stress types and the accent marks that show them",
  "Read the sound guide (CAPITALS for stress, Q for the glottal catch)",
  "Use the four facts about stress and accents"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Syllables: the beats of a word",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A **syllable** is one beat of a word. *ba-sa* has two beats, and *bu-lak-lák* has three. Knowing where the beats fall helps you say each word cleanly."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog has **four kinds of syllable**. V stands for a vowel and C for a consonant."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Kind",
      "Syllable",
      "In the word",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "V (one vowel)",
       "o",
       "[[táo|noun]]  tá-o",
       "person"
      ],
      [
       "V",
       "a",
       "[[paá|noun]]  pa-á",
       "foot"
      ],
      [
       "V",
       "i",
       "[[iyák|noun]]  i-yák",
       "cry"
      ],
      [
       "V",
       "u",
       "[[úlo|noun]]  ú-lo",
       "head"
      ],
      [
       "CV (consonant + vowel)",
       "ba or sa",
       "[[basa|pred]]  ba-sa",
       "read"
      ],
      [
