      ]
     ],
     "title": "The four kinds of syllable"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "No hard and fast rules",
     "t": "There is no simple rule for where every syllable starts and ends. At first, just memorise the stress together with each word as you learn it."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Rules that help",
     "items": [
      "A syllable never has more than **three** letters, and **ng** counts as one. So it is **ku-mus-tá** (how are you), never ku-must-á. **Pang** in pang-ápat (fourth) and **ngin** in hángin (wind) are only three sounds each.",
      "Do not separate a starting consonant from its vowel and leave a VC syllable in the middle of a word: **bu-má-nat** (to strike), not bum-á-nat.",
      "VC syllables are mostly at the start of words: **an-táy** (wait), **ak-yát** (climb). They can also come at the end after a vowel: **pa-ít** (bitter), **la-ot** (sea).",
      "Prefixes and suffixes \"grab\" sounds from the root: **bu-lak-lák** (flower) becomes **bu-lak-lá-kin** (flowery)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Sound crisp, not breathy",
     "items": [
      "Say Tagalog words faster, sharper and less breathy than English, more like Spanish.",
      "Do not puff air after p, k and t.",
      "Avoid long English a, e and o sounds. Someone who speaks Tagalog with an American accent is called **\"slang\"**."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Stress: the loud part of a word",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Stress** is the syllable you say with extra force. In Tagalog, a different stress can make a different word. The textbook puts an **accent mark** over a vowel to show which syllable is stressed and how."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Stress type",
      "Mark",
      "What you do",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "a. End stress",
       "acute ´ on the last vowel",
       "stress the **last** syllable",
       "[[anák|noun]]  (a-NAK)",
       "child"
      ],
      [
       "b. Penultimate stress",
       "acute ´ on the next-to-last vowel",
       "stress the **next-to-last** syllable",
       "[[babáe|noun]]  (ba-BA-e)",
       "woman"
      ],
      [
       "c. Penultimate stress with a glottal catch",
       "grave ` on the last vowel",
       "stress the next-to-last syllable, then close the throat at the end",
       "[[punò|noun]]  (PU-noQ)",
       "tree"
      ],
      [
       "d. End glottal catch (no stress)",
       "circumflex ^ on the last vowel",
       "say it faster, ending with a glottal catch; the stress seems lost",
       "[[punô|pred]]  (pu-noQ)",
       "full"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Four types of stress"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Reading the sound guide",
     "items": [
      "**CAPITAL letters** show the stressed syllable, as in ba-BA-e.",
      "**Q** stands for the **glottal catch**: an abrupt closing of the throat that blocks the air, like the break in \"uh-oh\". The textbook uses Q the same way.",
      "Q is only a sign. Do not say a q or k sound. Beginners often mistake the glottal catch for a k.",
      "Lower-case guides such as **rey-na** just show the syllables where the textbook marks no stress."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Long words",
     "t": "Longer words may have more than one stressed syllable, such as **pinagkákaguluhán** (being mobbed) and **mapágsamantalâ** (opportunistic)."
    }
   ]
  },
